PAGES=[page('099_page_2.jpg',190,[heading('7.4.2 A linear equation in two variables is a straight line'),paragraph('A first degree polynomial p₁(x) = a₁x+a₀ is rearranged to obtain an equation of the form'),eq(r'-a_1x+p_1(x)-a_0=0,\quad\mathrm{with}\ -a_1=a,p_1(x)=y,-a_0=b'),eq(r'ax+by+c=0'),paragraph('is then called the general equation of the straight line. Here a, b and c are constants while x and y are variables.'),heading('Remember'),paragraph('The first degree polynomial p₁(x) is also called the linear algebraic equation and is denoted by p₁(x) = f(x).'),paragraph('Consider, If P(x₁,y₁), Q(x₂,y₂) and R(x₃,y₃) are the three points on the locus represented by the straight line'),eq(r'ax+by+c=0','i'),paragraph('then, P(x₁,y₁) on the line (i) gives:'),eq(r'ax_1+by_1+c=0','ii'),paragraph('Q(x₂,y₂) on the line (i) gives:'),eq(r'ax_2+by_2+c=0','iii'),paragraph('R(x₃,y₃) on the line (i) gives:'),eq(r'ax_3+by_3+c=0','iv'),paragraph('The three lines from equation (ii) to equation (iv) develops a homogeneous system of three linear equations in the unknowns a, b and c:'),{'type':'matrixProduct','matrices':[ROWS,[['a'],['b'],['c']],[['0'],['0'],['0']]],'operators':['',' = '],'suffix':'⇒ Ax = 0 (v)','caption':'Source homogeneous three-by-three system'},paragraph('The homogeneous system of linear equations (v) defines a nontrivial solution only if the determinant of coefficient matrix A of the system (v) is zero:'),eq(r'det(A)=0'),{'type':'determinant','rows':ROWS,'suffix':'= 0 (vi)','caption':'Coordinate determinant for three collinear points'},eq(r'x_1(y_2-y_3)-y_1(x_2-x_3)+(x_2y_3-x_3y_2)=0'),paragraph('Equation (vi) is rearranged to obtain:'),eq(r'x_1(y_2-y_3)+x_2(y_3-y_1)+x_3(y_1-y_2)=0','vii'),paragraph('Multiply both sides of equation (vii) by 1/2 to obtain the area of the triangle formed by P, Q and R that equals zero:'),eq(r'\frac12[x_1(y_2-y_3)+x_2(y_3-y_1)+x_3(y_1-y_2)]=0'),eq(r'\mathrm{Area\ of\ the\ triangle}\ PQR=0','viii'),paragraph('Since the three points P, Q and R lying on the locus (i) are collinear. Hence, the locus (i) represents a straight line.'),note('A genuine line requires (a,b) ≠ (0,0). Otherwise 0x+0y+c = 0 gives the whole plane when c = 0, or no points when c ≠ 0. In the opening rearrangement y = a₁x+a₀, the consistent coefficients are a = −a₁, b = 1, c = −a₀; the printed assignment −a₀ = b does not match the following general form. A polynomial is a function; an equation is an equality involving it. The coordinate determinant gives signed twice-area; geometric area is its absolute value divided by 2. Zero still proves collinearity. The source uses x in Ax = 0 for the coefficient column (a,b,c), distinct from the point coordinate x.'),heading('7.4.3 General form of a straight line is reducible to other standard forms'),paragraph('Any standard form of a line can also be determined from the general form of a line (i).'),heading('i. To reduce the general form (i) to the slope intercept form of a line, we need to involve the following steps:'),eq(r'ax+by+c=0'),eq(r'by=-ax-c')]),page('100_page_1.jpg',191,[eq(r'y=-\frac abx-\frac cb'),eq(r'=mx+c_1,\quad\mathrm{slope}=m=-\frac ab,\quad y\mathrm{-intercept}=c_1=-\frac cb'),heading('ii. To reduce the general form (i) to the double-intercept form, we need to involve the following steps:'),eq(r'ax+by+c=0'),eq(r'ax+by=-c'),eq(r'\frac{ax}{-c}+\frac{by}{-c}=1,\quad\mathrm{dividing\ by}\ -c'),eq(r'\frac x{-c/a}+\frac y{-c/b}=1'),eq(r'\frac x{a_1}+\frac y{b_1}=1,\quad x\mathrm{-intercept}=a_1=-\frac ca,\quad y\mathrm{-intercept}=b_1=-\frac cb'),heading('iii. To reduce the general form (i) to the normal form, we need to involve the following steps:'),paragraph('From the Figure 7.18(b), the angles along the positive directions of the x and y-axis are the following:'),eq(r'\cos\theta=\frac pm,\quad\sin\theta=\frac pn'),paragraph('The values of cos θ and sin θ are used in the trigonometric identity cos²θ+sin²θ = 1 to obtain:'),eq(r'\cos^2\theta+\sin^2\theta=1'),eq(r'\frac{p^2}{m^2}+\frac{p^2}{n^2}=1'),eq(r'p^2\left(\frac1{m^2}+\frac1{n^2}\right)=1','ix'),eq(r'p^2\left(\frac{m^2+n^2}{m^2n^2}\right)=1'),eq(r'p^2=\frac{m^2n^2}{m^2+n^2}\ \Rightarrow\ p=\pm\frac{mn}{\sqrt{m^2+n^2}}'),paragraph('This p is the perpendicular distance from the origin to the line x/m+y/n = 1 (i.e. nx+my−mn = 0). Of course, the perpendicular distance from the origin to the line ax+by+c = 0 must be:'),eq(r'p=\frac{|c|}{\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}','x'),paragraph('For converting the general form (i) to normal form, divide the line ax+by+c = 0 by 1/√(a²+b²) to obtain the conversion of the general form (i) in the normal form:'),eq(r'\frac{ax}{\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}+\frac{by}{\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}+\frac c{\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}=0','xi'),note('Slope-intercept conversion requires b ≠ 0. The displayed double-intercept divisions require a,b,c all nonzero; a line through the origin does not have this divided form. The symbols m,n in the normal proof are intercepts, not slopes. As an ordinary distance p is nonnegative, so use |mn|/√(m²+n²), not an arbitrary ± value. The last instruction says divide by 1/√(a²+b²), but its displayed result actually divides by √(a²+b²). Normalize the coefficient vector (a,b), then reverse its sign when needed so that the right-hand side p is nonnegative: for c ≠ 0 multiply the equation by −sign(c)/√(a²+b²). If c = 0 either unit normal gives p = 0. The condition (a,b) ≠ (0,0) is essential.')])]
